index 0000000..e673c9f
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/en/administration/idle-compaction-experimental.md
@@ -0,0 +1,45 @@
+# Experimental periodic compaction (candidate patch)
+
+Ordinary compaction is triggered by IO. If it is skipped because another
+compaction is busy, no durable retry is queued. A file that becomes cold can
+therefore retain obsolete slice references indefinitely, even with trash
+disabled. Deleting unreferenced slices cannot remove these still-referenced
+objects; compaction must first replace the chunk's slice list.
+
+This candidate adds `--compact-interval 1h` to mount options. The default is
+`0` (disabled); read-only and `--no-bgjob` mounts never start the task. This
+is a **new option in the patched client**, not an existing release option.
+Do not pass it to an unpatched client.
+
+After an interval, one worker walks the mounted subtree, making one ordinary
+(non-force) compact attempt per chunk. It retains the existing admission and
+transactional origin checks. Busy/failed work and histories larger than 1000
+records are revisited in later passes, including after client restart, without
+a volatile queue or one goroutine per write. Completion requires a surviving
+enabled client and eventually successful traversals and storage operations.
+This does not bound capacity under indefinitely sustained overload.
+
+## Deployment boundaries
+
+- Initially enable on **one designated maintenance client only**, preferably
+  scoped with `--subdir`. No persistent volume format change is made.
+- A per-subtree metadata counter provides best-effort cross-client throttling,
+  not a lease: long passes and overlapping subtree mounts can overlap.
+- Walking large directories, hard links and huge sparse files adds metadata
+  work. Compaction rewrites object data even when the expected space saving is
+  small. Measure scan cost and foreground impact before production use; no
+  default activation or universal interval is proposed here.
+- Cancellation stops admission of further chunks, but cannot interrupt an
+  already-running object-compaction callback. A hung storage callback may delay
+  unmount. This patch does not change the object API's cancellation semantics.
+- A traversal status of zero means the tree walk completed; it does **not** mean
+  every compaction succeeded. Object failures are logged by the existing
+  compactor and retained candidates can be retried in a later pass.
+- Trash retention and deletion concurrency retain their existing meanings.
+  Space reclamation may lag slice-list reduction. Observe both metadata and
+  object-store capacity, and verify data integrity.
+
+This is an upstream discussion candidate, not production acceptance. It needs
+real-storage, restart, multi-client and scan-overhead validation in addition to
+unit regression tests. Existing `juicefs compact --threads 1 <exact-file>` is
+the manual maintenance fallback; it is not evidence of native automatic recovery.
